{"name":"Carriage Door","hex":"#958d79","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"carriage door","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A door that is reminiscent of the looks and styles of the original carriage house doors.","A door on a railway carriage."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/carriage_door"}],"entries":[{"title":"Carriage","blocks":[{"text":"A carriage is a two- or four-wheeled horse-drawn vehicle for passengers. Coaches are a special category within carriages; they are enclosed carriages with four corner posts and a fixed roof. From the early modern period until the rise of railways and motor vehicles, carriages were a principal means of road transport, with models ranging from simple vehicles to elaborate ceremonial coaches used by royalty. Although largely replaced by mechanized transport, carriages remain in use for ceremonial occasions, tourism, equestrian sport, and in some communities as everyday transport. Modern carriages have been redesigned and manufactured for sports such as combined driving and for tourist transport."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Horse_of_Buckingham_Palace%2C_Londres_cropped_straightened.jpg/330px-Horse_of_Buckingham_Palace%2C_Londres_cropped_straightened.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carriage","wikidata":"Q235356"},{"title":"Door","blocks":[{"text":"A door is a hinged or otherwise movable barrier that allows ingress (entry) into and egress (exit) from an enclosure. The created opening in the wall is a doorway or portal. A door's essential and primary purpose is to provide security by controlling access to the doorway (portal). Conventionally, it is a panel that fits into the doorway of a building, room, or vehicle. Doors are generally made of a material suited to the door's task. They are commonly attached by hinges, but can move by other means, such as slides or counterbalancing."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/L-door.png/330px-L-door.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Door","wikidata":"Q36794"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Concours_national_d%27attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg/500px-Concours_national_d%27attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Concours_national_d%27attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg/1280px-Concours_national_d%27attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Concours_national_d%27attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg/500px-Concours_national_d%27attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Competitive driving in France","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AConcours_national_d'attelage_%C3%A9lite_Rennes_2014-1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sal%C3%A3o_Nobre%2C_Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches%2C_Lisboa%2C_Portugal.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Sal%C3%A3o_Nobre%2C_Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches%2C_Lisboa%2C_Portugal.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sal%C3%A3o_Nobre%2C_Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches%2C_Lisboa%2C_Portugal.tif/lossy-page1-1280px-Sal%C3%A3o_Nobre%2C_Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches%2C_Lisboa%2C_Portugal.tif.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Sal%C3%A3o_Nobre%2C_Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches%2C_Lisboa%2C_Portugal.tif/lossy-page1-500px-Sal%C3%A3o_Nobre%2C_Museu_Nacional_dos_Coches%2C_Lisboa